1. Introduction
One important data structure is the Heap, which is commonly used to implement priority queues and efficient sorting algorithms.
This report introduces the Heap data structure, including its abstract data type (ADT), operations. 
The report also explains the Heap Sort algorithm, analyzes its time complexity, and demonstrates how the algorithm works through examples.

Heap Data Structure
A heap tree data structure is a special type of complete binary tree in which all levels are completely filled except possibly the last level. 
The nodes in the last level are filled from left to right.

2. A heap satisfies two main properties:
Structural Property – The heap must be a complete binary tree.
Heap Property – The relationship between a parent node and its child nodes must follow a specific ordering rule.

Complete Binary Tree
A complete binary tree is a tree where:
•	All levels are completely filled except possibly the last level.
•	The last level is filled from left to right.

2.2 Types of Heap
Max Heap
In a Max Heap, the value of each parent node is greater than or equal to its children.
The largest element is always at the root.
Min Heap
In a Min Heap, the value of each parent node is smaller than or equal to its children.
The smallest element is always at the root.

3. Heap Abstract Data Type (ADT)
The Heap ADT defines the operations that can be performed on a heap structure.
Operation	Description
insert(x)	Insert a new element into the heap
extractMax() / extractMin()	Remove and return the root element
peek()	Return the root element without removing it
heapify()	Adjust the heap to maintain the heap property

4. Array Representation of Heap Sort
A heap is stored in an array instead of using explicit tree pointers.
If a node is stored at index i in the array:
•	The left child is located at index:
2i + 1
•	The right child is located at index:
2i + 2
•	The parent node is located at index:
(i - 1) / 2
This representation makes heap operations such as insertion and deletion efficient, since the tree structure can be easily maintained using array indices.
